From 5977019bef0e92f05e7fc1019f55210ea1e44955 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Mathias Karstaedt <mathias.karstaedt@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 8 Sep 2026 19:03:51 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 2/2] BleKeyboardHost: don't run the page-turner fallback on
 boot-keyboard frames

The generic fallback exists for remotes that are not boot keyboards. Its guard
accepted any device exposing a Consumer Control page, which is nearly every
modern keyboard, since media keys live there.

The result on an ordinary keyboard: each key RELEASE produces a keyboard-shaped
report that emits no new key, so !emittedKb held and the fallback scanned that
frame for a representative code. On a keyboard carrying a constant byte in its
report -- the Keychron K2 HE holds 0x39 in byte 2 of every frame, including the
all-released one -- every release surfaced as a phantom key event. Measured: a
steady stream of End keypresses between real keystrokes, which would move the
cursor on its own as soon as a firmware acts on navigation keys.

Requiring !keyboardShaped fixes it without narrowing genuine remote support: a
frame that parsed as a boot keyboard was already handled above, and emitting
nothing there means the key was released, not that the code is unknown.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 .../network/BleKeyboardHost/src/BleKeyboardHost.cpp | 13 ++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 12 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/libs/network/BleKeyboardHost/src/BleKeyboardHost.cpp b/libs/network/BleKeyboardHost/src/BleKeyboardHost.cpp
index 4e66ec4..4b898ec 100644
--- a/libs/network/BleKeyboardHost/src/BleKeyboardHost.cpp
+++ b/libs/network/BleKeyboardHost/src/BleKeyboardHost.cpp
@@ -760,7 +760,18 @@ void BleKeyboardHost::onReportIngest(const uint8_t* data, size_t len) {
   // page or place the code at a non-standard byte. When the keyboard slots produced
   // nothing and the device doesn't look like a pure keyboard, scan the report for a
   // representative code and surface it (edge-detected so one press == one event).
-  const bool tryGeneric = !emittedKb && (g_hasConsumerPage || !g_hasKeyboardPage || n < 7);
+  //
+  // Requires !keyboardShaped. Nearly every modern keyboard also exposes a
+  // Consumer Control page for its media keys, so g_hasConsumerPage alone let the
+  // heuristic run on ordinary boot-keyboard frames. Every key RELEASE produces a
+  // keyboard-shaped report that emits nothing, the fallback then scanned that
+  // frame for a "representative code", and a keyboard with a constant byte in
+  // its report (the Keychron K2 HE holds 0x39 in byte 2 of every frame) turned
+  // each release into a phantom key event. A frame that parsed as a boot
+  // keyboard has already been handled by the code above; emitting nothing there
+  // means "the key was released", not "unknown device code".
+  const bool tryGeneric =
+      !emittedKb && !keyboardShaped && (g_hasConsumerPage || !g_hasKeyboardPage || n < 7);
   if (tryGeneric) {
     size_t codeIdx = 0;
     const uint8_t code = extractPrimaryCode(p, n, &codeIdx);
